# LOG ROTATION for the Burnticker host's logs (2026-10-18 prelaunch audit A-32: none existed; the publish log reached 9.9 MB in a week).
# A template: scripts/ops/install-burnticker-units.sh fills in the repository directory, the service user and its group
# (scripts/ops/render-logrotate.mjs), checks the result with `logrotate -d` and writes it to ~/.config/burnticker/logrotate.conf, which
# mct-burnticker-logrotate.service runs daily as the service user (the units are user units, so the system logrotate never sees these
# files) with its own state file.
#
# su: logrotate skips, with only a warning, a directory that is group- or world-writable unless it is told whose files these are; naming
# the service user and group keeps rotation working whatever the mode of logs/.
# copytruncate: the units append to these files through systemd (StandardOutput=append:), which holds them open and cannot be told to
# reopen; the file is copied, then truncated in place, and the next append lands at its start. A line written in that instant can be lost.
@REPO@/logs/*.log {
    su @USER@ @GROUP@
    daily
    rotate 14
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
}
